WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.720
Your line now joins a wideband line,

2
00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:05.440
sixteen thousand samples a second,
and you build the bridge.

3
00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:06.820
Then the tickets arrive.

4
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:09.120
A second voice under every voice,
thin and high.

5
00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:11.940
One customer says it goes down
when her mother's voice goes up.

6
00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:13.390
You test it.

7
00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:14.990
A colleague hums one thousand hertz

8
00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:17.510
into a frequency meter
on the wideband side.

9
00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:18.670
The hum arrives at half height.

10
00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:21.410
And a second tone,
just as strong, at seven thousand.

11
00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:22.810
Slide the hum down to five hundred,

12
00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:24.150
and the ghost slides up,

13
00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:25.590
to seven thousand five hundred.

14
00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:28.410
Your line can't even store seven thousand.

15
00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:29.510
Where does it come from?

16
00:00:29.530 --> 00:00:30.430
Look at the bridge.

17
00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:33.610
It passes each sample through
and puts a zero between every two,

18
00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:36.550
so eight thousand samples
a second become sixteen thousand.

19
00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:37.890
That's upsampling by two.

20
00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:39.470
The zeros look like silence.

21
00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:41.160
Lesson three's wheel.

22
00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:44.500
A tone is a turning dot,
and a sample is its left-right shadow.

23
00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:46.240
Twice the samples, half the turn.

24
00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:48.880
One thousand hertz turns
twenty-two and a half degrees.

25
00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:51.000
Seven thousand turns
one hundred fifty-seven and a half,

26
00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:53.320
under half a turn,
so here it's a real tone.

27
00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:55.390
Push the hum through the bridge.

28
00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:57.290
Even samples sit on the true tone.

29
00:00:57.310 --> 00:00:58.370
Odd samples are zero.

30
00:00:58.390 --> 00:00:59.750
Now add half of one thousand hertz

31
00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:01.170
to half of seven thousand.

32
00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:03.210
On even samples the halves
agree and add up.

33
00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:05.950
On odd samples they're opposite,
and cancel to zero.

34
00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:07.210
Exactly the bridge output.

35
00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:08.540
Why?

36
00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:10.080
Each step of the seven thousand dot

37
00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:11.400
is a flip, a half turn,

38
00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:13.100
then twenty-two and a half degrees back.

39
00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:15.380
On even samples the flips
pair up into whole turns,

40
00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:17.860
so it sits at the hum's mirror spot,
the same shadow.

41
00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:20.880
On odd samples one flip is left,
so the shadow is opposite.

42
00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:23.920
So the zeros were never silence.

43
00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:27.400
They're your tone at half height,
plus a second tone, called an image.

44
00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:29.560
Upsampling makes one for every tone,

45
00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:31.540
at eight thousand hertz minus the tone,

46
00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:33.120
mirrored across four thousand.

47
00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:36.360
A whole voice comes back upside down,
from four thousand six hundred

48
00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:38.320
to seven thousand seven hundred hertz.

49
00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.560
After the zeros,
the bridge needs a low-pass filter,

50
00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:42.820
gain two below four thousand

51
00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:45.040
to undo the halving, and zero above.

52
00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:47.640
Averaging a gap's two neighbors
slopes too gently.

53
00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:49.520
A longer rule steps more steeply.

54
00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:50.920
Thirty-one samples keeps the voice,

55
00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:52.460
and every image under eight percent.

56
00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:55.600
Going down,
the bridge keeps every other sample.

57
00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:56.840
That's downsampling.

58
00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:58.420
The hiss of an S, at six thousand hertz,

59
00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:00.700
lands on your line
as a two thousand hertz squeal,

60
00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:02.720
an alias across the same mirror.

61
00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:04.400
So the low-pass filter goes first,

62
00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:05.700
gain one, while six thousand

63
00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:06.960
is still six thousand.

64
00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:09.940
Upsampling never adds detail.

65
00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:12.240
The best bridge adds
almost nothing above four thousand,

66
00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:14.280
and ours leaves a faint seven percent.

67
00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:15.400
Then a new ticket.

68
00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:16.820
A thin whine on every call,

69
00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:19.200
exactly four thousand hertz,
on the mirror line.
